{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "import math\ndef newman_prime(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the Newman-Shanks-Williams number at index 'n'.\n    It returns the NSW number corresponding to the given index n.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1  # NSW_0 = 1\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1  # NSW_1 = 1\n\n    nsw_k_minus_2 = 1  # Corresponds to NSW_0\n    nsw_k_minus_1 = 1  # Corresponds to NSW_1\n\n    # Iterate from k = 2 up to n\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        current_nsw_num = 2 * nsw_k_minus_1 + nsw_k_minus_2\n        nsw_k_minus_2 = nsw_k_minus_1\n        nsw_k_minus_1 = current_nsw_num\n    \n    # After the loop, nsw_k_minus_1 holds the value for NSW_n.\n    # The problem implies that 'n' will correspond to an NSW number that is prime.\n    # No error handling for non-prime results as per constraints.\n    return nsw_k_minus_1"}
