{"task_id": "Mbpp/440", "solution": "import re\nimport string\ndef _clean_word(word: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Removes leading and trailing punctuation from a given word string.\"\"\"\n    return word.strip(string.punctuation)\ndef _is_adverb(word: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Determines if a given cleaned word is an adverb using a simple heuristic.\"\"\"\n    # This function uses a common heuristic (ending in 'ly') for adverb detection.\n    # For robust production systems, a linguistic POS tagger would be used.\n    return word.lower().endswith('ly') and len(word) > 2\ndef find_adverb_position(sentence: str) -> tuple[int, int, str] | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first adverb and its 0-indexed character positions in a given sentence.\n    Returns (start_position, end_position, adverb_text) or None if no adverb is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    for match in re.finditer(r'\\S+', sentence):\n        raw_word = match.group(0)\n        raw_start = match.start()\n\n        cleaned_word = _clean_word(raw_word)\n\n        if _is_adverb(cleaned_word):\n            # Calculate the length of leading punctuation that was stripped\n            leading_punct_len = 0\n            for char_idx, char in enumerate(raw_word):\n                if char in string.punctuation:\n                    leading_punct_len += 1\n                else:\n                    break\n            \n            adverb_start = raw_start + leading_punct_len\n            adverb_end = adverb_start + len(cleaned_word)\n            \n            return (adverb_start, adverb_end, cleaned_word)\n\n    return None"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/445", "solution": "def index_multiplication(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs index-wise multiplication of elements in two nested tuples.\n    It assumes inputs are well-formed tuples of tuples containing numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_outer_tuple = []\n    for inner_tuple_a, inner_tuple_b in zip(tuple1, tuple2):\n        result_inner_tuple = []\n        for element_a, element_b in zip(inner_tuple_a, inner_tuple_b):\n            result_inner_tuple.append(element_a * element_b)\n        result_outer_tuple.append(tuple(result_inner_tuple))\n    return tuple(result_outer_tuple)"}
