{"task_id": "Mbpp/635", "solution": "def heap_sort(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of comparable elements using the heap sort algorithm.\n    Returns a new list with elements sorted in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    arr = list(input_list) # Create a copy to avoid modifying the original list\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    def _heapify(arr: list, n: int, i: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Maintains the max-heap property for a subtree rooted at index i.\n        This is a private helper function for heap_sort.\n        \"\"\"\n        largest = i  # Initialize largest as root\n        left = 2 * i + 1  # left child\n        right = 2 * i + 2  # right child\n\n        # See if left child of root exists and is greater than root\n        if left < n and arr[left] > arr[largest]:\n            largest = left\n\n        # See if right child of root exists and is greater than current largest\n        if right < n and arr[right] > arr[largest]:\n            largest = right\n\n        # Change root, if needed\n        if largest != i:\n            arr[i], arr[largest] = arr[largest], arr[i]  # swap\n            _heapify(arr, n, largest) # Heapify the root.\n\n    def _build_max_heap(arr: list):\n        \"\"\"\n        Builds a max-heap from the given list in-place.\n        This is a private helper function for heap_sort.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Build a maxheap.\n        # Since last parent will be at ((n//2)-1) we can start from there.\n        for i in range(n // 2 - 1, -1, -1):\n            _heapify(arr, n, i)\n\n    _build_max_heap(arr) # Build the initial max-heap\n\n    # One by one extract elements\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        arr[i], arr[0] = arr[0], arr[i]  # Swap\n        _heapify(arr, i, 0) # Heapify root element to get highest element at root again\n\n    return arr"}
